        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='width:100%;min-height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;container-type:inline-size;background:#ffffff;font-family:Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 clamp(20px,5cqw,40px) 64px'><div style='padding-top:32px'><header data-role='masthead'><div style='padding:10px 0;border-top:3px solid #0f172a;border-bottom:1px solid #0f172a;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>NAVIGATION · WHERE WE ARE</div><h1 style='margin:40px 0 0;font:500 clamp(40px,9.5cqw,72px)/.98 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.025em;text-wrap:balance;color:#0f172a'>What a click does today</h1><p style='margin:18px 0 0;max-width:36ch;font:italic 400 clamp(20px,3.6cqw,26px)/1.35 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#475569'>Read from the code and set against the agreed scheme. The differences are the work.</p></header></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-start;gap:32px 40px'><div style='flex:999 1 380px;min-width:0'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>How to read this</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><span style='float:left;padding:6px 10px 0 0;font:500 68px/54px Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'>T</span>he scheme says what a click should do. This page says what it does now, so that the gap between the two can be built in a sensible order.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Everything here was read from the code, not found by using the app. Where I did not trace something to the end, it says so. Each difference has three lines: what happens today, what the scheme says, and what the work is.</p></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0'><section data-role='settings' style='padding-top:10px;border-top:3px solid #0f172a'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0f172a'>About this page</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;column-gap:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #cbd5e1'><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Read from</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>The app’s code, not from using it</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Read on</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>12 October 2026</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Set against</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>A click means closer</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Built since</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>Steps 1 to 5 on 12 October 2026: the node ladder, the tile ladder and stepping out, one history with the trail, going through, and travel along edges. Step 5 has not yet been seen running.</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Not traced</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>What a click on empty canvas does beyond clearing the selection</dd></dl></section></div></div></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>What already matches</h2><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Back remembers where you were.</strong> The workspace keeps a history of stops, and each stop is a graph, a node and a camera position. Back restores all three.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Going through opens beside,</strong> for an account with the workspace. The graph you left stays open as its own tile.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Opening a graph that is already open lands on it.</strong> It does not get a second copy.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>A Portal has a control in its title bar</strong> that opens its graph beside this one. That is the way through the scheme asks for when editing.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>A pinned thing pans when dragged by its body,</strong> and moves by its title bar.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>A drag never navigates,</strong> and a click never moves anything.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Step out exists,</strong> from a graph to the workspace.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>What differs</h2><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Clicking a graph’s tile</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> A click on a tile’s body or title bar selects it and centres its card, at the zoom you are already at. It does not enter. Entering takes the Focus button in the title bar, or a click on one of the nodes showing inside it.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> Far away, the camera comes to it and frames it. In view, a click enters it. The title bar is what selects without entering.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Make the body’s click frame, then enter. Leave selection to the title bar. Remove the need for a Focus button.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Clicking a node</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> One click selects the node and glides the camera so the node is centred. The zoom does not change, so a node that was an icon is still an icon, now in the middle of the screen. A second click does nothing more.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> Three steps: the camera comes to it; it takes focus and is brought to reading size; then you are inside it.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> This is the largest change. Focus has to set the distance as well as the position, and a third step, being inside, has to exist as a state the app knows about.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Going through a Portal</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> A click on a Portal does what it does on any node. Going through takes the open control in its title bar, or a double-click, which opens whatever a node names as its source.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> The third click goes through. When editing, the title-bar control or Ctrl-click.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Add the third step. The title-bar control stays as it is.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Stepping out</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> Step out goes from a graph to the workspace, and frames everything. There is no step from a node back to its graph. Escape clears the selection.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> One step at a time, node to graph to workspace, and Escape does the same.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Add the node-to-graph step, and bind Escape.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Two Backs</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> There are two histories. The address bar has its own back and forward, over the addresses it has shown. The workspace has Back and Forward over its stops. They do not know about each other.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> One way back, which returns you to the same graph, node and distance.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Make the address bar’s arrows and the workspace’s Back one thing, built on the workspace’s history, which already records what the scheme needs.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Accounts without the workspace</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> A free account has a single slot. Opening a graph replaces the one that was open, and the workspace keeps no history for it.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> Going through never closes anything, and the app keeps the way back.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Decided by Michael on 12 October 2026: it behaves as a web browser does. One graph at a time; going through puts you wholly in the other graph; the graph’s border is never seen; Back returns you to the end of the connection you left. So the single slot stays, and what is new is a history of stops for it, which a free account does not have today.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Ctrl-click</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> On a node, Ctrl-click is selection: it adds to or takes from what is selected, and does not travel. On a link in text, Ctrl-click and Shift-click open a new browser tab.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> Ctrl-click opens what you clicked beside this graph and leaves you where you are. Shift-click is selection. Middle click is a new tab.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Move multi-selection to Shift, and give Ctrl-click its new meaning everywhere.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Double-click</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> Double-clicking a node opens its source, if it has one. Double-clicking an edge opens its properties, for someone who can edit.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> Decided by Michael on 12 October 2026: double-click is the end of the line. It does the last step for a thing at once, from any distance. A Portal that is only an icon or a mark is gone through; a node is entered; a tile is entered.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Give double-click that one meaning everywhere. Opening a node’s source goes, unless the node is a way through. Opening an edge’s properties when editing fits, since when editing the last step is to edit.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Trunks, handles and ports on a tile</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> A click on a trunk makes its owner the graph you are in and selects the Bridge or Portal node the edge belongs to. An edge that leads to a graph that is not open is drawn as a stub, and a click on the stub opens that graph beside. The handle bars and the ports on a tile’s border do not answer a click.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> A trunk frames both graphs, then selects a stroke, then travels to the far end. A handle travels to the graph it faces. A border port travels to the node that owns it.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Most of this is new. The stub’s behaviour already fits.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>The trail</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> There is none. The address bar shows one address.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> A line near the address bar naming the graphs you came through, each a way back to it.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> New, and it can be drawn straight from the workspace’s history.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Walking</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> No key walks from node to node, and the arrow keys are bound to nothing. A port’s label can be clicked to follow its edge to the other end. (An older key handler, which nudged nodes with Ctrl and the arrows and moved the selection with Tab, is still in the code but nothing uses it.)</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> The arrow keys walk along the edge leaving the node in focus, and back.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> New on the keys. The label click is the same idea and can share its code. Nothing else is bound to the arrow keys.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Ways back drawn into graphs</h3><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Today:</strong> The two canon indexes and the three room demos each carry a Portal back to the root, placed by hand.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The scheme:</strong> The app keeps the way back. No graph draws one.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The work:</strong> Take them out, once Back and the trail hold.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>A build order</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Each step can be seen working before the next begins.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>1. The node ladder.</strong> Come to it, focus at reading size, inside. Everything else in the scheme leans on focus meaning a distance as well as a place.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>2. The tile ladder, and stepping out.</strong> A click frames, then enters. Step out goes one level at a time. Escape steps out.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>3. One history, and the trail.</strong> The address bar and the workspace share one Back. An account without the workspace gets the same history for its single slot, so that Back returns it to the end of the connection it left. The trail is drawn from it.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>4. Going through.</strong> A Portal’s third click, links, Ctrl-click to open beside without travelling, and double-click as the end of the line.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>5. Travel along edges.</strong> Trunks, handles, border ports, and edges inside a graph.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>6. Walking.</strong> The arrow keys.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>7. Take the ways back out of the graphs.</strong></li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Needs a decision before it is built</h2><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The five questions at the end of the scheme:</strong> one click or two for a Portal, how many graphs stay open, whether editing needs a different third step, what the trail shows on a loop, and whether walking belongs on the arrow keys.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Where this was read</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The files each finding came from, for whoever builds the change.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Tiles, selection and focus: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>docs/multi-graph-workspace.md</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/components/WorkspaceLayer.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Node focus and the camera: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>handleNodeFocus</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/handlers/graphEditorHandlers.js</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/focusTransition.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Portals: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>handleUnfoldGraph</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>handleNavigate</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>handlePrimaryAction</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/Nodes/PortNode.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Double-click: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>handleNodeDoubleClickOpenSource</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/GraphEditorContent.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Opening beside or replacing: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>navigateTo</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/hooks/useGraphWorkspace.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>History and Step out: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>recordNavigation</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>navigateWorkspaceHistory</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>stepOut</code> in the same file</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Links: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/components/TlzLink.js</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/components/pageLinkClick.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Keys: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/hooks/useGraphShortcuts.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Following a port’s label: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/NodeGraph/portLabelNavigation.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Which gesture belongs to what: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/NodeGraph/interactionArbiter.js</code></li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:40px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:stretch;gap:28px 32px'><div style='flex:1 1 190px;min-width:0'><section data-role='route' data-prominence='primary' style='height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding-top:12px;border-top:3px solid #6d28d9'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:500 26px/1.1 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#0f172a'>The scheme</h2><p style='margin:0 0 10px;font:400 14px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>A click means closer: what each click should do.</p><div style='margin-top:auto;font:700 13px/20px system-ui;color:#6d28d9'>Open the scheme →</div></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 190px;min-width:0'><section data-role='route' data-prominence='secondary' style='height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding-top:14px;border-top:1px solid #0f172a'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:500 22px/1.1 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#0f172a'>The plan</h2><p style='margin:0 0 10px;font:400 14px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>What’s next, where this work is tracked.</p><div style='margin-top:auto;font:700 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Open the plan →</div></section></div></div></div></div></div>"
        }
      },
