        "title": "What differs",
        "body": "## Clicking a graph’s tile\n\n- **Today:** A click on a tile’s body or title bar selects it and centres its card, at the zoom you are already at. It does not enter. Entering takes the Focus button in the title bar, or a click on one of the nodes showing inside it.\n- **The scheme:** Far away, the camera comes to it and frames it. In view, a click enters it. The title bar is what selects without entering.\n- **The work:** Make the body’s click frame, then enter. Leave selection to the title bar. Remove the need for a Focus button.\n\n## Clicking a node\n\n- **Today:** One click selects the node and glides the camera so the node is centred. The zoom does not change, so a node that was an icon is still an icon, now in the middle of the screen. A second click does nothing more.\n- **The scheme:** Three steps: the camera comes to it; it takes focus and is brought to reading size; then you are inside it.\n- **The work:** This is the largest change. Focus has to set the distance as well as the position, and a third step, being inside, has to exist as a state the app knows about.\n\n## Going through a Portal\n\n- **Today:** A click on a Portal does what it does on any node. Going through takes the open control in its title bar, or a double-click, which opens whatever a node names as its source.\n- **The scheme:** The third click goes through. When editing, the title-bar control or Ctrl-click.\n- **The work:** Add the third step. The title-bar control stays as it is.\n\n## Stepping out\n\n- **Today:** Step out goes from a graph to the workspace, and frames everything. There is no step from a node back to its graph. Escape clears the selection.\n- **The scheme:** One step at a time, node to graph to workspace, and Escape does the same.\n- **The work:** Add the node-to-graph step, and bind Escape.\n\n## Two Backs\n\n- **Today:** There are two histories. The address bar has its own back and forward, over the addresses it has shown. The workspace has Back and Forward over its stops. They do not know about each other.\n- **The scheme:** One way back, which returns you to the same graph, node and distance.\n- **The work:** Make the address bar’s arrows and the workspace’s Back one thing, built on the workspace’s history, which already records what the scheme needs.\n\n## Accounts without the workspace\n\n- **Today:** A free account has a single slot. Opening a graph replaces the one that was open, and the workspace keeps no history for it.\n- **The scheme:** Going through never closes anything, and the app keeps the way back.\n- **The work:** Decided by Michael on 12 October 2026: it behaves as a web browser does. One graph at a time; going through puts you wholly in the other graph; the graph’s border is never seen; Back returns you to the end of the connection you left. So the single slot stays, and what is new is a history of stops for it, which a free account does not have today.\n\n## Ctrl-click\n\n- **Today:** On a node, Ctrl-click is selection: it adds to or takes from what is selected, and does not travel. On a link in text, Ctrl-click and Shift-click open a new browser tab.\n- **The scheme:** Ctrl-click opens what you clicked beside this graph and leaves you where you are. Shift-click is selection. Middle click is a new tab.\n- **The work:** Move multi-selection to Shift, and give Ctrl-click its new meaning everywhere.\n\n## Double-click\n\n- **Today:** Double-clicking a node opens its source, if it has one. Double-clicking an edge opens its properties, for someone who can edit.\n- **The scheme:** Decided by Michael on 12 October 2026: double-click is the end of the line. It does the last step for a thing at once, from any distance. A Portal that is only an icon or a mark is gone through; a node is entered; a tile is entered.\n- **The work:** Give double-click that one meaning everywhere. Opening a node’s source goes, unless the node is a way through. Opening an edge’s properties when editing fits, since when editing the last step is to edit.\n\n## Trunks, handles and ports on a tile\n\n- **Today:** A click on a trunk makes its owner the graph you are in and selects the Bridge or Portal node the edge belongs to. An edge that leads to a graph that is not open is drawn as a stub, and a click on the stub opens that graph beside. The handle bars and the ports on a tile’s border do not answer a click.\n- **The scheme:** A trunk frames both graphs, then selects a stroke, then travels to the far end. A handle travels to the graph it faces. A border port travels to the node that owns it.\n- **The work:** Most of this is new. The stub’s behaviour already fits.\n\n## The trail\n\n- **Today:** There is none. The address bar shows one address.\n- **The scheme:** A line near the address bar naming the graphs you came through, each a way back to it.\n- **The work:** New, and it can be drawn straight from the workspace’s history.\n\n## Walking\n\n- **Today:** No key walks from node to node, and the arrow keys are bound to nothing. A port’s label can be clicked to follow its edge to the other end. (An older key handler, which nudged nodes with Ctrl and the arrows and moved the selection with Tab, is still in the code but nothing uses it.)\n- **The scheme:** The arrow keys walk along the edge leaving the node in focus, and back.\n- **The work:** New on the keys. The label click is the same idea and can share its code. Nothing else is bound to the arrow keys.\n\n## Ways back drawn into graphs\n\n- **Today:** The two canon indexes and the three room demos each carry a Portal back to the root, placed by hand.\n- **The scheme:** The app keeps the way back. No graph draws one.\n- **The work:** Take them out, once Back and the trail hold.",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "navigation-today"
        }
      },
